format 1
adapter blog 1
unit: inside-the-atom

post blog-the-smallest-thing
author: npc-hal
lesson: atoms-are-everywhere
title: The smallest thing you will ever meet
hook: You have never seen one, yet you are made of them. Let us look at how small they really are.
cover: atom
read: 2
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch1
references: ref-openstax-chem-ch2

p:
    Look at your hand. Now look at the air around it. Both are matter, and all matter is made of atoms.

p:
    Yes, even the air, which gives every impression of being nothing. That is why a pumped-up ball is heavier than a flat one: the air inside it is matter too. I have weighed several balls to be certain. The neighbours have asked me to stop.

quote:
    Small things are best looked at slowly. I still look at them every day.

h: How small is small?

p:
    Take a skin cell. You need a microscope to see it. A virus is about 300 times smaller than that cell. And an atom is about 300 times smaller again.

figure: atom
caption: An atom: far too small to see, even with an ordinary microscope.

list:
    Your hand: about 10 cm
    A skin cell: about 0.03 mm
    A virus: about 100 nm
    An atom: a few tenths of a nm

fact:
    About 2,400 years ago the Greek thinker Democritus imagined cutting something in half again and again, until he reached a piece that could not be cut. He called it atomos. Seeing atoms took far longer.

p:
    Light and heat are different. They are energy, not matter, so they are not made of atoms.

p:
    So the next time you pick something up, remember that you are holding a huge crowd of atoms you can never see. Take a moment with that. I will wait.

next: Zoom in on an atom yourself => lesson atoms-are-everywhere

